<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hengistbury Head</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hengistbury Head is a headland on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> Harbour, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>. In the 1st century BCE it was one of the main ports of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a> and receiving Italian wine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a>, while exporting metals, grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Excavations there have also found much older remains, including a late Ice Age hunters' camp.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hengistbury-head.jpg" alt="Hengistbury Head" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hut 8.5 at en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hengistbury_Head.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hengistbury Head, formerly also called Christchurch Head, is a sandstone headland joined to Southbourne, a suburb of Bournemouth, and marks the eastern end of Poole Bay; its high ground is called Warren Hill. The Mudeford spit, a sand bar running from it, closes Christchurch Harbour from the south. Long part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, it was placed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> by the Local Government Act 1972. The name was recorded as Hedenesburia in an early 12th-century deed, and the antiquary Francis Grose wrongly linked it in 1779 with the Jutish leader Hengist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>An open-air camp of the Creswellian culture, dated to around 14,100 years ago, has yielded more than 13,000 stone artefacts and is probably the largest site of its period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> hunter-gatherers used the headland once the sea had flooded the valley below. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> it was an important seaport, and its round barrows held axes, cremation urns and the high-status burial of a young woman with amber, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> alloy and gold.</p><p>Around 700 BCE a settlement was founded and the headland was cut off by the Double Dykes, two banks and ditches. Its people smelted iron and refined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from silver-bearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, and thousands of bronze coins of the Durotriges suggest a mint. More North Italian wine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> have been found here than at all other sites of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> together; most date from before 50 BCE, and the trade seems to have declined about the time of Caesar's campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><p>Iron-rich stones from the headland went into Christchurch Castle in the 11th century. Andrew Yarranton's harbour pier of 1693 failed, and from 1848 to 1872 the Hengistbury Mining Company quarried the ironstone boulders that hold the headland together, which with later erosion cost it up to a third of its area. In 1910 the first international aviation meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was held there, during which Charles Rolls was killed, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the army occupied it with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a> station.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bournemouth Borough Council bought the headland in 1930 to keep it as open space. It is a Scheduled Ancient Monument and a Local Nature Reserve, declared in 1990, and part of the Christchurch Harbour Site of Special Scientific Interest; a visitor centre displaying its archaeology opened in December 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hengistbury Head” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their harbour at <b>Hengistbury Head</b> traded with Armorica in the 1st century BCE, bringing in Italian wine amphorae and Gaulish pottery in exchange for metals, grain and cattle.</blockquote><a href="/state/durotriges">Durotriges</a></li></ul></section>
